      <section class="section-block evidence-caution" aria-labelledby="evidence-caution-title">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <div><p class="kicker">Read before believing</p><h2 id="evidence-caution-title">Don’t crown a winner yet.</h2></div>
          <p>These numbers describe the calls we’ve seen so far. They are clues, not a verdict on which model is “the best”.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="limitations-grid">
          <article><h3>Read the sample size</h3><p>Models can have different numbers of scored calls. A 60% rate on five calls is a very different claim from 60% on fifty — always read the counts next to the percentages.</p></article>
          <article><h3>Same war, same rounds</h3><p>Models often bet on the same front in the same hours. Twenty calls from one war are not twenty independent experiments — one weird week can flatter or sink anyone. The aggregate uses each model’s available rounds descriptively; it is not a paired comparison on shared, mature cases.</p></article>
          <article><h3>Timing has a catch</h3><p>Average timing error can only be computed when something actually happened, so it quietly ignores false alarms. The bullseye rate keeps those misses in view; the custom CRPS-style table below combines timing, outcome, and stated probability.</p></article>
        </div>
      </section>
